From: pavel@PARK.bu.edu (Paul Cisek)
Date: Thu, 22 Sep 1994 11:41:49 -0400
Subject: [#94-9-369] Functional motivation for evolution? - Penrose's "Shadow..."

>From: GREG NEWTON <gnewton@ozemail.com.au>
>X-Message-Number: #94-9-365
>
>From: gnewton@ozemail.com.au(Greg Newton)
>
>It is interesting to read Paul Ciseks' article replying to Doug Cutrell, were 
>he mentions the lack of a "functional motivation for evolution of such a 
>mechanism"  - my understanding of evolution is that there is no "functional 
>motivation" required, as this implies the existence of a goal or a motivated 
>party (a creator). Evolution is a "dumb" process of mutations being naturally 
>selected by the environment at the time the mutation occurs.  Therefore *any* 
>possible mutation can occur and whether it continues or not depends on the 
>local environmental conditions from that time onwards.

Let me try to make my terminology clearer (Karl is chuckling somewhere...).

When I say "functional motivation" I do not imply a motivation in a
goal-seeking sense but in a survival value sense.  An evolutionary innovation
is "motivated" by its contribution to the reproductive success of its source
genes.  It's a usage that seemed reasonable to me at the time.

Please give me some benefit of the doubt.  Scientific fields often borrow
terminology from other existing fields or from natural conversation, without
intending to bring along all of the meanings those terms carry in their
original contexts.  This is unfortunate, since it leads to misinterpretation.
Conversations about evolution are especially susceptible to causing such
misunderstandings, since the only good words for describing what occurs happen
to be ones that normally imply a motivated party.  However, it would
be difficult to make progress in even a lightly technical discussion
if we constantly require `backward compatibility' to issues which
should already have been laid to rest.

>Also I fail to see that 
>primitive organisms (without consciousness) can "solve problems in order to 
>survive" - they can only rely on mutations (which cannot be induced 
>"artificially" by creatures without conciousness), natural selection makes the 
>rules, and determines who/what wins the game.

Again, we define "solve" differently.  My meaning comes from the usage of
"solution" as any scheme for dealing with some task at hand.  Such a
"solution" may be "discovered" by the brute-force "search" that results
from the evolutionary process.  (I've put in quotes other words that
one might be tempted to antropomorphize; please don't).  This again
implies no goal-seeking.  I would guess that your definition of "solve"
carries implications of some rational process that depends on consciousness.
If so, then I question the utility of your definition, since it relies
upon assumptions made about phenomena that we do not understand.  What
exactly are "creatures without consciousness"?

>From: pgf@srl02.cacs.usl.edu (Phil G. Fraering)
>X-Message-Number: #94-9-351
>
>...please consider this: a
>trait only needs to confer a 5% survival advantage in order to become
>ubiquitous in a population over a reasonably short time

Good point, but we still have not been presented with even a .01% advantage
of Penrose's constructs.  And even if we were, the advantage itself is not
enough.  It has to be reachable from our current phenotype state.  The process
cannot evaluate mechanisms that it can't construct - hence no wheeled
creatures (until recently :-) ).  The process can't jump very far along the
fitness landscape to find better optima, it's stuck to a great extent with
choices made in the past.

Paul


