I see one real issue and one strawman here. The strawman is that 
systems designed "by hand" are necessarily fully understood. A little
practicum in large-scale software projects, or an examination of
the pitiful state of  proof of correctness technology will show that
we routinely build systems that are beyond the ability of any one of
us to comprehend.

The real issue, which applies to all systems but especially, as you
point out to things like genetic algorithms, is building confidence
in from the beginning. This is one reason why "AI" programs like
planners and rule-based expert systems are built with tracing --
when you get a conclusion you can ask where it came from.

Things like neural nets and GAs are often built for applications 
where the correctness of the answer is "obvious" once it's found,
anyway -- in fact that's the essence of GA, that you have a fitness
test built in to choose among plausible solutions. But where this is
not true, or where the output will be used without a human in the loop,
secondary verifications need to be built in, their rigor depending,
of course, on the risk attendant on a wrong answer.

