From: Webb_S <WebbS@oplc.psb.bls.gov>
Date: Thu, 22 Sep 94 12:48:00 EDT
Subject: [#94-9-374] Quantum Erasers

On Tue, 20 Sep 1994, Ray wrote:

>   It's a fine theory (having been invented plenty of times before),
> the question is, does it lead us to anything new? If a new interpretation
> of quantum mechanics doesn't lead to any new measurable predictions,
> it's useless as physics. It becomes theology. One of the neat things
> about the Many Worlds hypothesis is that it actually does make
> a testable prediction that would fail under the Copenhagen interpretation.
> Find a way to transmit information faster than light in QM, and you'll
> be famous.

Speaking of which, I just finished reading _Cosmic Wormholes_ by Paul 
Halpern.  Apparently, according to Kip Thorne et al., objects near a 
wormhole lack well-defined positions and velocities until an observation is 
made.  In such cases, the sum over histories approach to measurement, 
previously reserved for QM situations involving small particles, seems 
appropriate.  In other words, near wormholes we have QM on a macroscopic 
scale.  Thus, interestingly, it seems that while QM itself may not provide 
us with a way to transmit information faster than light, any situation which 
would allow such transmission must operate by QM rules.

Ray continues -

>   So the question is, if the universe simulator distributes the
> result of a measurement all over the universe before processing
> the next one, can we manipulate or predict the outcome of the
> experiment deterministically? How does your interpretation
> explain the randomness in QM?

Halpern's book got me thinking about randomness in QM as well.  Thorne, 
Morris, et al. assert in a recent paper that "the only solution to the laws 
of physics that can occur locally in the real universe are those which are 
globally self-consistent."  In other words, any action or motion occurring 
over a short distance or short period of time must be consistent with the 
behavior of the entire cosmos, for beginning to end.  Since wormholes are a 
generally accepted feature of the spacetime "foam", I wondered: could it be 
that the very existence of backward time travel, by requiring that every 
observation of the universe be consistent with its entire evolution, be 
selecting the results which are observed in quantum measurements?  This 
seems somewhat like the Superdeterminist interpretation of QM, except that 
it would be inaccurate to say that the measurement outcomes are 
predetermined, since they would depend as much on the future as the past.

I'm no physicist, but it seems that this sort of thing should be 
mathematically provable.  The theory would then be validated if and when 
backward time travel is ever realized.  While it may not allow us to do away 
with quantum uncertainty, it might at least explain the source of the 
apparent randomness in QM.

