From: doug@OpenMind.com (Doug Cutrell)
Date: Thu, 22 Sep 1994 12:04:03 -0700
Subject: [#94-9-378] SCI: Role for Q.M. in neurobiology

Paul Cisek writes:
(X-Message-Number: #94-9-369)

>... we still have not been presented with even a .01% advantage
>of Penrose's constructs.  And even if we were, the advantage itself is not
>enough.  It has to be reachable from our current phenotype state.  The process
>cannot evaluate mechanisms that it can't construct - hence no wheeled
>creatures (until recently :-) ).  The process can't jump very far along the
>fitness landscape to find better optima, it's stuck to a great extent with
>choices made in the past.

First, these are by no means "Penrose's" constructs.  The basic ideas for
quantum mechanical processes involved in neurobiology are actually quite
old, dating back at least to 1968 with a paper by Frolich.  The ideas have
been kicked around a lot in recent years by such people as Ian Marshall and
Karl Pribram.

Second, these putative Q.M. processes could easily have been an intrinsic
component of the normal functioning of cells from very early on in
evolution... tracing back to the very origins of eukaryotic life, for
example.  Speaking as a biologist (a title which I can loosely claim), I
can assure you that the details of intracellular organization are some of
the least well understood subjects in molecular biology.  The interior of
eukaryotic cells in particular is a mind-boggling maze of dynamic
structures and within-cell coordinating processes that is almost impossible
to study with our current experimental toolsets.  The biologists whom I
have respected the most, have been keen to impress upon me how little we
understand, rather than how much.  The interior of the cell is by no means
a "bag" of freely mixing components, but rather a fantastically complex
active matrix.  This is all within-cell, and the exact mechanisms at play
here all have to do with molecular-level interactions.

I see no argument against the evolution very early on of some QM-coherent
long range interactions (still intracellular, long range on the *molecular*
level) as participating in the overall control and functioning of this
matrix.  Note that it was not until the evolution of the eukaryotic cell
type that multi-cellular life forms were able to evolve.  Perhaps the early
mechanisms for intracellular organization based on some QM process (e.g.
the microtubule hypothesis) laid the groundwork for extended structures
which coordinate *between* cells.

It is not necessary to assume that "neural networks" came first, and that
some Q.M. process was only tacked on later when it was needed.  It could be
that the functioning of the brain is only based on neural networks at a
gross macroscopic scale, and that these underlying processes have *always*
been present.  In fact, I would predict that *if* something of this sort is
shown to be occurring in vertebrate brains, that it also occurs in some
fashion throughout the evolutionary tree.

This is ALL highly speculative.  My intent is not to convince anyone of
anything here -- except, perhaps, that all possibilities are open.

Doug

